        "sourceExtract": "<div style=\"font-weight: 700; color: #b45309; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Official progress report published by General Inspector of German Highways Dr. Fritz Todt, celebrating state-directed infrastructure as a triumph of employment creation (1936).</div><div>The Reichsautobahn is not merely an engineering marvel; it is our primary weapon in conquering mass unemployment. By putting hundreds of thousands of German men to work with spades and pickaxes, we have restored the German worker to his rightful place of honor. Every kilometre of concrete laid represents a triumph of national willpower over economic defeat.</div>",
        "modelAnswer": "Source B is highly useful for investigating economic policies because it documents how public works were used to project a spectacle of recovery, celebrating the Autobahn as \"our primary weapon in conquering mass unemployment\". Todt framed manual construction as an honorable duty that \"restored the German worker to his rightful place of honor\", boosting national pride even though only 125,000 men were directly employed on road-building. As an official progress report by the General Inspector of Highways in 1936, its purpose was to glorify state infrastructure, offering authentic evidence of how the regime manufactured working-class consent through visible engineering triumphs.<br><br>Source C is equally useful because it exposes the harsh economic reality and increased exploitation behind official employment figures, recording that \"average weekly working hours increased from 43 hours in 1933 to 49 hours by 1939\". This statistical reality shows that full employment was bought through severe sacrifice: with independent trade unions banned, the German Labour Front (DAF) prevented wage bargaining, leaving take-home pay \"eroded by compulsory deductions for the DAF and Winter Relief\". As an objective statistical survey of payroll data, its purpose was industrial planning, providing reliable, unvarnished proof that living standards declined as the economy shifted toward rearmament.",
